Fix false positive duplicate linker script error on Windows hosts (upstream bug ld/34540).

On Windows (MSVCRT/UCRT), stat() sets st_ino to 0 and st_dev to the drive number (e.g. 2 for C:).
Checking !((a).st_ino == 0 && (a).st_dev == 0) erroneously evaluates to true for any file on
a non-A: drive, causing ANY two distinct linker scripts on the same drive (e.g. main.ld including
sub.ld or multiple -T options) to be falsely flagged as duplicate files:
"error: linker script file 'sub.ld (main.ld)' appears multiple times".

Keying the inode availability guard on st_ino != 0 correctly identifies that inode info is
unavailable on Windows CRT stat(), restoring the correct behavior where distinct scripts are
not falsely flagged as identical.

--- a/include/same-inode.h
+++ b/include/same-inode.h
@@ -29,8 +29,8 @@
 # elif defined _WIN32 && ! defined __CYGWIN__
    /* Native Windows.  */
-   /* stat() and fstat() set st_dev and st_ino to 0 if information about
+   /* stat() and fstat() set st_ino to 0 if information about
       the inode is not available.  */
 #  define SAME_INODE(a, b) \
-    (!((a).st_ino == 0 && (a).st_dev == 0) \
+    ((a).st_ino != 0 \
      && (a).st_ino == (b).st_ino && (a).st_dev == (b).st_dev)
 # else
